---
import ProductLayout from '../layouts/ProductLayout.astro';
import Shot from '../components/Shot.astro';

const description = 'Rehearse PostgreSQL restores, author semantic validation profiles and independently review original recovery evidence in Nostekon Studio or CI.';

const install = `git clone https://github.com/jagarkarlo/nostekon.git
cd nostekon
NOSTEKON_PORT=8180 docker compose up --build -d`;

const report = `go build -o /tmp/nostekon-report ./cmd/nostekon-report
/tmp/nostekon-report --suite examples/suites/postgresql-policy \\
  > /tmp/nostekon-suite-review.json`;

const steps = [
  { title: "Describe the drill", text: "A YAML file names the failure to simulate, where to restore, how deep to verify and your recovery time and data loss goals.", state: "Available", later: false },
  { title: "Run the restore", text: "The lab backs up seeded PostgreSQL, deletes its source namespace and restores into a second k3d cluster. A policy suite exercises zero loss, strict tail loss and budgeted loss.", state: "Lab only", later: true },
  { title: "Record what happened", text: "Original case JSON, a suite summary and a host-side write ledger preserve the measured outcomes. Terminal host jobs can export a checksummed evidence bundle.", state: "Available", later: false },
  { title: "Evaluate the evidence", text: "The Go engine evaluates each case independently and gates the whole suite. Studio compares the summary with those results and exports the same review as the CLI.", state: "Available", later: false },
  { title: "Check who holds the key", text: "Sign original evidence with the CLI, authenticate the owner's public-key fingerprint, and verify the exact bytes in Studio or the browser. Local revocation stays separate from the recovery verdict.", state: "Available", later: false },
];
---
<ProductLayout title="Restore evidence and policy suites" description={description} current="overview">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Nostekon</h1>
      <p class="lead">Find out whether a restore really worked. Rehearse a PostgreSQL backup, check what came back, and keep original evidence you can independently evaluate again.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/demo/index.html#/suite">Open the policy suite</a>
        <a class="btn" href="#quickstart">Run the local app</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note">Pre-alpha. Real captures from two disposable k3d clusters. The browser demo never contacts a cluster; lab execution in the packaged app is disabled by default.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" aria-label="Screenshot">
    <Shot name="policy-suite" height={900} alt="Nostekon policy suite with three recorded V4 outcomes: zero loss verified, strict tail loss failed as expected, and budgeted tail loss verified. The independently evaluated suite gate passes." caption="Three real lab captures, one independent policy gate. The strict zero-loss policy fails on two lost writes, as expected; the budgeted policy accepts the measured loss. Signatures remain unverified." />
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>What a report tells you</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">One verdict, and the reasons behind it.</p>
      </div>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div>
          <dt>How deep recovery went</dt>
          <dd>Five levels, from "the backup job succeeded" to "the data is correct". The report names the deepest level that passed in order, and the first check that failed.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>How long it took</dt>
          <dd>Recovery time runs from the moment of failure to completion, split into phases, with any time no phase accounts for shown separately.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>How much data was lost</dt>
          <dd>If you record acknowledged writes, Nostekon compares them with what came back and reports the loss window and any gaps in the sequence.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>What it cannot tell you</dt>
          <dd>A report judges the evidence it is given. A signature shows the file was not changed afterwards. It does not show the runner told the truth. <a class="link" href="/evidence/">More on the evidence model</a>.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Keep the evidence, not just a green result</h2><p class="section-lead">From a backup to a review you can reproduce.</p></div>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>Rehearse an existing backup</dt><dd>Restore a bounded PostgreSQL custom-format archive into a disposable local database. Check snapshot row counts and optional semantic rules. <a class="link" href="/docs/guides/postgresql-backup-rehearsal/">Backup rehearsal guide</a>.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Choose the checks that matter</dt><dd>Author not-null, uniqueness and foreign-key profiles in Studio, then export exact JSON for the runner. Validating a profile checks its structure, not your data. <a class="link" href="/demo/index.html#/profiles">Author a validation profile</a>.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Reopen and compare originals</dt><dd>Save runs, compare outcomes and transfer original evidence with attached plans or signatures. Imported files never grant trust, and a valid signature never overrides a failed recovery. <a class="link" href="/demo/index.html#/runs">Browse recovery evidence</a>.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Use the same gate in CI</dt><dd>The browser and Go CLI independently evaluate the same inputs. A complete expected policy suite passes; incomplete or mismatching evidence does not get a green gate. <a class="link" href="/docs/guides/k3d-isolated-restore/">Suite workflow</a>.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>How a drill runs</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">The whole flow, with what exists today marked.</p>
      </div>
      <ol class="steps">
        {steps.map((step) => (
          <li class="step">
            <h3>{step.title}</h3>
            <span class:list={["step-state", "pipeline-state", { later: step.later }]}>{step.state}</span>
            <p>{step.text}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="quickstart">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Try it on your machine</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">Start with the packaged app or captured evidence. Neither needs a Kubernetes cluster.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="code-grid">
        <div class="code-card quickstart-step">
          <h3>1. Start the local app</h3>
          <p>Requires Git, Docker and Docker Compose. The container serves Studio and the Go API together on localhost, without cluster credentials or a Docker socket.</p>
          <div class="code-block">
            <div class="code-head"><span>Shell</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy-target="quickstart-install" data-copy-label="Copy app setup commands" aria-label="Copy app setup commands">Copy</button></div>
            <pre><code id="quickstart-install">{install}</code></pre>
          </div>
          <p class="code-note">Open <a class="link" href="http://127.0.0.1:8180/#/suite">127.0.0.1:8180</a>. Lab execution is off. Stop the app with <code>docker compose down</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="code-card quickstart-step">
          <h3>2. Gate a recorded suite</h3>
          <p>From the same checkout, use Go 1.25+ to evaluate the included three-case capture. This creates a review without starting a restore or contacting Kubernetes.</p>
          <div class="code-block">
            <div class="code-head"><span>Shell</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy-target="quickstart-report" data-copy-label="Copy suite gate commands" aria-label="Copy suite gate commands">Copy</button></div>
            <pre><code id="quickstart-report">{report}</code></pre>
          </div>
          <p class="code-note">The recorded suite exits 0, including its intentional strict-policy failure. JSON goes to <code>/tmp/nostekon-suite-review.json</code>; invalid input exits 2.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="code-card quickstart-step">
          <h3>3. Run a disposable restore</h3>
          <p>For a new capture, follow the isolated lab walkthrough. It requires Docker, k3d, kubectl, Python 3.12+ and Go, and deletes only the dedicated lab namespaces.</p>
          <p><a class="link" href="/docs/guides/k3d-isolated-restore/">Set up the two-cluster lab</a></p>
          <p class="code-note">Execution requires explicit host opt-in. The review container cannot run drills. Production recovery orchestration is not supported; custom-backup rehearsals use the separate Python CLI.</p>
          <p><a class="link" href="/docs/start/">CLI setup, trusted-key verification and runtime details</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="prose" style="margin-top: 24px;">Prefer not to install anything? <a class="link" href="/demo/index.html#/suite">Open the policy suite</a>. The demo evaluates evidence; it does not connect to Kubernetes. Browser history is local to its origin, not a shared or permanent evidence store.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Where it stands</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="split">
        <div>
          <h3>Works today</h3>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>Validate drill files from the CLI, the API or Studio</li>
            <li>Rehearse bounded PostgreSQL custom-format backups with snapshot counts and semantic checks</li>
            <li>Author validation profiles in Studio and independently review bound restore plans</li>
            <li>Run a three-case PostgreSQL policy suite across two local k3d clusters, checked through V4</li>
            <li>Review, save and reopen suite snapshots with original-file downloads</li>
            <li>Verify portable host-job bundles and independently gate captured suites in CI</li>
            <li>Evaluate evidence: level reached, recovery time by phase, exact data loss from a write ledger</li>
            <li>Sign with the CLI; verify in Studio or the browser using explicit public-key trust and revocation</li>
            <li>Push recovery metrics to Prometheus and chart them on a bundled Grafana dashboard</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Not built yet</h3>
          <ul class="ticks todo">
            <li>Scheduled drills</li>
            <li>Production recovery orchestration and complete application recovery</li>
            <li>Business-specific checks beyond not-null, uniqueness and foreign keys</li>
            <li>Collecting evidence from the system itself instead of trusting the runner</li>
            <li>Authenticated operator identity and trusted signing timestamps</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top: 36px;">
        <p>Nostekon does not orchestrate backups and does not replace a backup tool. It exercises the restore you already have and reports whether it actually worked. See the <a class="link" href="/docs/comparison/">alternatives page</a> for how that differs from a sandbox restore or a health check, and the <a class="link" href="/roadmap/">roadmap</a> for what comes next.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
</ProductLayout>

<script>
  document.addEventListener("click", async (event) => {
    if (!(event.target instanceof Element)) return;
    const button = event.target.closest<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-copy-target]");
    if (!button) return;
    const target = document.getElementById(button.dataset.copyTarget ?? "");
    if (!target) return;
    const label = button.dataset.copyLabel ?? "Copy";
    try {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(target.textContent?.trim() ?? "");
      button.textContent = "Copied";
      button.setAttribute("aria-label", "Commands copied");
    } catch {
      button.textContent = "Copy failed";
      button.setAttribute("aria-label", "Clipboard unavailable");
    }
    window.setTimeout(() => {
      button.textContent = "Copy";
      button.setAttribute("aria-label", label);
    }, 1600);
  });
</script>
